{"country":"united states","collectioncode":"ncsm-unio","countrycode":"usa","county":"colbert-lauderdale","flags":["dwc_order_replaced","dwc_originalnameusageid_added","gbif_reference_added","dwc_taxonrank_replaced","dwc_taxonomicstatus_added","gbif_genericname_added","dwc_taxonid_added","gbif_taxon_corrected","dwc_multimedia_added","dwc_scientificnameauthorship_added","gbif_vernacularname_added","gbif_canonicalname_added","idigbio_isocountrycode_added","dwc_parentnameusageid_added","dwc_datasetid_replaced"],"locality":"tennessee river, river mile 242-245, [points estimated 5.36 air miles ene to 7.25 air miles ese center cherokee], [cherokee and pride quads].","recordset":"cf93f9ef-b45e-474c-9ca9-3edc4ad11a4d","geopoint":{"lon":-87.8818,"lat":34.7776},"occurrenceid":"457b757e-e1d7-11ec-80ba-06f21c000156","scientificname":"dreissena polymorpha","stateprovince":"alabama","uuid":"ff0c1268-c4aa-49cb-9d99-d4580577b407","taxonid":"2287072","taxonomicstatus":"accepted","catalognumber":"44334","basisofrecord":"preservedspecimen","canonicalname":"dreissena polymorpha","taxonrank":"species","institutioncode":"ncsm"}}],"attribution":[{"uuid":"b7804802-37ce-47fe-8b6f-0d2eea4e2747","itemCount":5,"name":"Invertebrate Zoology Collections at the Museum of Biological Diversity (OSUM)","description":"The Division of Invertebrate Zoology at The Ohio State University Museum of Biological Diversity is a major center for research on the evolution, ecology, taxonomy and conservation of global invertebrate biodiversity. We maintain several important natural history collections of invertebrates: Bivalve Mollusks, Gastropods (and other non-bivalve mollusks), Crustaceans, and General Invertebrates.\nThough we have specimens collected as early as the 1810s, the collections formally began in the 1890s as part of the \"O.S.U. Museum of Zoology\". The collections were then managed by the Ohio Historical Society (OHS) which operated on OSU's campus as \"The Ohio State Museum\" from about 1925 to the 1960s. In 1970, the University became the primary custodian of all the zoological collections aside from a small synoptic set of Ohio material retained by OHS. The invertebrate collections were historically curated independently as 3 separate Divisions of the museum, but have been been managed as one Invertebrate Zoology Division of the Museum of Biological Diversity since 2024. \nThe invertebrate zoology collections contain >1.5 million dry and >900,000 fluid-preserved specimens in ca. 125,000 cataloged lots. Our collecting and curatorial efforts are predominantly focused on documenting the worldwide distribution and diversity of non-marine (i.e. land and freshwater) invertebrates, specifically mollusks and crustaceans. We maintain the largest collection of freshwater bivalves in the world, and our gastropod and crayfish collections are also among the worlds' largest.","url":"https://invertebrates.osu.edu/","emllink":"https://asc-ipt.asc.ohio-state.edu/mbd/eml.do?r=osum_invertebrate_zoology","archivelink":"https://asc-ipt.asc.ohio-state.edu/mbd/archive.do?r=osum_invertebrate_zoology","contacts":[{"first_name":"Nathaniel","last_name":"Shoobs","role":"Curator","email":"shoobs.1@osu.edu"},{"first_name":"Nathaniel","last_name":"Shoobs","role":"Curator","email":"shoobs.1@osu.edu"},{"first_name":"Nathaniel","last_name":"Shoobs","role":"Curator","email":"shoobs.1@osu.edu"}],"data_rights":"Unknown License, assume Public Domain","publisher":"8263f60e-3283-4fce-b6ab-7b6b3268e452","totalCount":80334,"hitRatio":0.000062240147384669},{"uuid":"d5b27393-7d3f-4b91-aebe-9066f9b1562d","itemCount":4,"name":"Auburn University Museum of Natural History Mollusks","description":"The Auburn University Museum of Natural History Mollusk Collection consists of a wide range of taxa from all over the world. Our geographic foci lie in the Southeast U.S., with the great biodiversity of freshwater mussels in Alabama, and in Antarctica. We also have a strong representation of Oceania from two private shell collections donated over the years. The majority of our accessions have been through University research and affiliated persons. The Mollusk Collection contains representatives of most molluskan groups, with a heavy concentration in unionids.","url":"","emllink":"https://ipt.idigbio.org/eml.do?r=aum-mollusks","archivelink":"https://ipt.idigbio.org/archive.do?r=aum-mollusks","contacts":[{"first_name":"Emmy","last_name":"Delekta","role":"Collections Manager","email":"aumnhin@auburn.edu"},{"first_name":"Nusrat","last_name":"Noor","role":"Collection Manager","email":"njn0012@auburn.edu"},{"first_name":"Brianne","last_name":"Varnerin","role":"Collection Manager"},{"first_name":"Brianne","last_name":"Varnerin","role":"Collection Manager"},{"first_name":"Nusrat","last_name":"Noor","role":"Collection Manager","email":"njn0012@auburn.edu"},{"first_name":"Emmy","last_name":"Delekta","role":"Collections Manager","email":"aumnhin@auburn.edu"},{"first_name":"Emmy","last_name":"Delekta","role":"Collections Manager","email":"emd0083@auburn.edu"},{"first_name":"Emmy","last_name":"Delekta","role":"Collections Manager","email":"aumnhin@auburn.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"ab5bdf3a-6283-41c7-ac16-9ba46381aa24","totalCount":21399,"hitRatio":0.00018692462264591802},{"uuid":"cf93f9ef-b45e-474c-9ca9-3edc4ad11a4d","itemCount":3,"name":"NCSM Mollusk Collection","description":"The North Carolina Museum of Natural Sciences' Mollusks Collection is comprised of over half a million specimens that are 83% freshwater species (mussels, fingernail clams and snails), 10% marine species and 7% terrestrial species (snails).","url":"https://collections.naturalsciences.org/search/mollusks","emllink":"https://ipt.vertnet.org/eml.do?r=ncsm-unio","archivelink":"https://ipt.vertnet.org/archive.do?r=ncsm-unio","contacts":[{"first_name":"Ben","last_name":"Norton","role":"Head of Technology","email":"ben.norton@naturalsciences.org"},{"first_name":"Jamie","last_name":"Smith","role":"Collections Manager of Mollusks","email":"jamie.smith@naturalsciences.org"},{"first_name":"Art","last_name":"Bogan","role":"Research Curator, Mollusks","email":"arthur.bogan@naturalsciences.org"},{"first_name":"Ben","last_name":"Norton","role":"Head of Technology","email":"ben.norton@naturalsciences.org"},{"first_name":"Art","last_name":"Bogan","role":"Research Curator of Mollusks","email":"art.bogan@naturalsciences.org"},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Russell","role":"VertNet Programmer","email":"larussell@vertnet.org"},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Bloom","role":"VertNet Coordinator","email":"dbloom@vertnet.org"},{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Wieczorek","role":"Information Architect","email":"tuco@berkeley.edu"},{"first_name":"Jamie","last_name":"Smith","role":"Collections Manager of Mollusks","email":"jamie.smith@naturalsciences.org"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"842a2bb5-d705-4d6c-8401-abf3ca28c05d","totalCount":38072,"hitRatio":0.00007879806682076066},{"uuid":"97058091-eb35-401b-b286-18465761f832","itemCount":1,"name":"Delaware Museum of Nature and Science – Mollusks","description":"The Museum's mollusk collection consists of more than 2 million specimens, making it one of the largest in the United States. The 220,000 cataloged lots represent more than 18,000 species. Worldwide in scope and covering all seven living classes of mollusks, our holdings include marine gastropods (45%), land and freshwater gastropods (30%), marine bivalves (15%), freshwater bivalves (5%) and other (5%). The Museum's mollusk collection is primarily dry shells, with some alcohol preserved cephalopod specimens. Most specimens are recent; however there is some Cenozoic fossil material. Our type collection contains more than 1,200 lots.","logo":"https://invertebase.org/portal/content/collicon/dmnhmollusk.png","url":"http://www.delmns.org/mollusks/","emllink":"https://invertebase.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=10","archivelink":"https://invertebase.org/portal/content/dwca/dmnh-mollusk_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"egbot@asu.edu"},{"email":"egbot@asu.edu"},{"email":"eshea@delmns.org"},{"first_name":"Elizabeth","last_name":"Shea","email":"eshea@delmns.org"},{"first_name":"Alex","last_name":"Kittle","role":"Sr. Colln Mgr","email":"akittle@delmns.org"},{"first_name":"Elizabeth","last_name":"Shea","email":"eshea@delmns.org"},{"first_name":"Alex","last_name":"Kittle","role":"Sr. Colln Mgr","email":"akittle@delmns.org"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"4233dd84-4adf-4fa6-8eb6-153f8bc77a61","totalCount":235868,"hitRatio":0.000004239659470551325}],"aggs":{"rs":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"b7804802-37ce-47fe-8b6f-0d2eea4e2747","doc_count":5},{"key":"d5b27393-7d3f-4b91-aebe-9066f9b1562d","doc_count":4},{"key":"cf93f9ef-b45e-474c-9ca9-3edc4ad11a4d","doc_count":3},{"key":"97058091-eb35-401b-b286-18465761f832","doc_count":1}]},"unique_scientific_names":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"dreissena polymorpha","doc_count":13}]},"max_dm":{"value":1753375484764,"value_as_string":"2025-07-24T16:44:44.764Z"}}}